tors  still you would not expect that given  the enticing price Spain has sent the  seat leon ecomotive out onto the pitch  can it keep up with just three cylinders  turbo and direct injection offset the  loss of a cylinder and then some the 85  kilowatt 200 Newton meter gasoline  engine delivers quite a punch had it as  fuel efficient at the same time  six liters per 100 kilometers is a  realistic number in every day traffic  the leon ecomotive is a smooth performer  the three cylinders don't vibrate nor do  they sound like a lawnmower even the  entry-level version offers a sporty firm  right the workmanship is impeccable the  model may be four years old but it does  not look at all outdated it may come as  a surprise given their names but the Kia  Cee'd and the Hyundai i30 are true  Europeans developed and manufactured  year they may share a common platform  but they are far from identical twins  they are also competing against each  other for our favor the Kia Cee'd has a  lien somewhat sporty look the big round  I'd instrument panel looks friendly with  black and grey make an elegant combo to  play forward it is equipped with a 100  kilowatt diesel engine and 6-speed  manual transmission it is a match for  any in the class  the Hyundai i30 has a more restrained  and sober look than the Kia the interior  is ergonomic and well put together but  perhaps a little over modest but Hyundai  gets extra points for all his extras  that come as standard it is also a bit  cheaper than the Kia performance-wise  there is little to choose between the  one is built in the Czech Republic the  other in Slovakia that's why Kia and  Hyundai can compete in our championship  if these cars were soccer stars they  would be considered dribbling champions  it's hard to really differentiate  between them they all have electronic  stability control all are comfortable to  drive all have a slight tendency to  oversteer which is typical of compact  cars only the sea otters out for its  more 